course: biology
lesson: 171
title: The Southern Ocean: blue whales, colossal squid and life under the ice
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Southern Ocean is the cold, stormy sea that goes all the way round Antarctica, and a huge current races round and round it like water swirling round a bath plug.
explain: It looks empty and icy, but in spring tiny drifting plants turn the water green, tiny shrimp-like krill eat them, and the biggest animals that ever lived come to eat the krill.
explain: A blue whale opens its mouth, gulps a swimming-pool load of water full of krill, then pushes the water out through comb-like plates and swallows the krill, like straining pasta in a sieve.
explain: Deeper down live the colossal squid and the sperm whales that hunt squid, and even under thick ice shelves, far from open water, scientists have found fish, sponges and other animals.
fact: The blue whale is the biggest animal that has ever lived, bigger than any dinosaur; its heart is about the size of a bumper car at a fair, and its calls can travel hundreds of kilometres, but its throat is far too narrow to swallow anything as big as a person.
fact: Whales are not fish but mammals: they breathe air and feed their babies milk.
fact: The colossal squid lives in the deep Southern Ocean and is the heaviest animal without a backbone known; a young one was reported filmed alive in the wild for the first time in 2025.
fact: Sperm whales dive more than two kilometres deep and can hold their breath for over an hour to hunt squid, and squid suckers leave round scars on their skin.
fact: Humpback whales blow rings of bubbles around krill and fish to trap them, and their songs change over the years as whales across an ocean learn the new songs.
fact: The Antarctic Circumpolar Current flows round Antarctica and is the strongest ocean current in the world, carrying many times more water than all the world's rivers together.
fact: People hunted whales for oil and meat until the 1980s, when most whaling stopped; since then humpback numbers in some places have recovered greatly.
fact: Tiny drifting plants called phytoplankton bloom in the Southern Ocean in spring, and whale poo helps feed them because it contains iron they need.
fact: In the cold Southern Ocean some animals grow much bigger than their cousins in warm seas, such as sea spiders as big as a dinner plate, which take in oxygen through their legs.
fact: A starfish pushes its stomach out of its body onto its food and digests the food outside; many Antarctic starfish are big and live for many years.
fact: In 2021 scientists drilling through about nine hundred metres of ice found sponges living on a rock underneath, far from open water and sunlight.
fact: Elephant seals are the largest seals and, like other seals, have four flippers; the males have big noses like a short trunk, fight on beaches, and the seals can dive more than a kilometre and a half deep.
exercise: What is the biggest animal that has ever lived? | The blue whale | The colossal squid | The biggest dinosaur | The elephant seal
exercise: How does a blue whale catch krill? | It gulps water full of krill and strains the water out | It chases each little krill and bites it with its teeth | It stings the krill with long tentacles | It sucks krill up through its blowhole
exercise: What is special about the colossal squid? | It is the heaviest animal without a backbone known | It is the fastest fish in the sea, faster than a sailfish | It lives in warm shallow reefs | It is the only squid with a shell
exercise: Why do some sperm whales have round scars on their skin? | Suckers of the squid they hunt leave marks | They scrape themselves on the sharp sea ice | Sharks bite them in circles | They are born with spots
exercise: How long can a sperm whale hold its breath? | Over an hour | About one minute | About five minutes | A whole week
exercise: How do humpback whales use bubbles? | They blow rings of bubbles to trap krill and fish | They breathe the bubbles while they are underwater | They use bubbles to keep warm | They blow bubbles to clean their skin
exercise: What happens to humpback songs over the years? | They change, and whales across an ocean learn the new songs | They never change at all, the same song from year to year for ever | Each whale sings only one note | Only baby whales sing
exercise: What is the Antarctic Circumpolar Current? | The strongest ocean current, flowing round Antarctica | A warm river in the middle of Antarctica, under the ice | A wind that blows over the ice | A current that flows only in winter
exercise: When did most whaling stop? | In the 1980s | In the 1800s | It has never stopped anywhere | Last year
exercise: What makes the Southern Ocean turn green in spring? | Tiny drifting plants called phytoplankton | Green seaweed washed off the rocky shores of the islands | Melting green ice | Paint from ships
exercise: How does whale poo help the ocean? | It holds iron that tiny drifting plants need | It warms up the cold water around the whales | It makes the ice melt faster | It scares sharks away
exercise: How do sea spiders take in oxygen? | Through their legs | Through gills on their heads | By coming up to breathe air | Through their mouths
exercise: How does a starfish eat? | It pushes its stomach out and digests food outside its body | It chews its food with rows of sharp teeth hidden under each of its arms | It filters water through its arms | It swallows food through a hole in each arm
exercise: What did scientists find under about nine hundred metres of ice in 2021? | Sponges living on a rock | Nothing at all, only ice | A frozen dinosaur | A lost ship
exercise: Which are the largest seals? | Elephant seals | Harbour seals | Fur seals | Leopard seals
exercise: How deep can elephant seals dive? | More than a kilometre and a half | About ten metres | About a hundred metres, like a diver | They cannot dive at all, they only float
test: In a cartoon like Finding Nemo two fish get swallowed and swim around inside a blue whale's mouth. What is real? | A blue whale gulps water and strains out krill, then swallows only small food | Blue whales swallow big fish whole, and even small sharks, every single day | Blue whales chew sharks with huge teeth | Blue whales eat only seaweed
test: «my mate says blue whales are smaller than the biggest dinosaurs» What is right? | No, the blue whale is the biggest animal that has ever lived | Yes, dinosaurs were always bigger | Yes, the blue whale is only a medium-sized animal | No, sharks are the biggest animals that have ever lived in the sea
test: «3 elephant seals on the beach, how many flippers» | 12 | 6 | 9 | 8
test: «5-armed starfish and 2 sea spiders with 8 legs each, how many arms n legs altogether» | 21 | 13 | 18 | 26
test: What would happen if all the whales in the Southern Ocean disappeared? | Less whale poo would mean less iron for the tiny plants at the bottom of the food chain | The sea would freeze solid | Krill would turn into fish | The current round Antarctica would stop
test: «whales are just big fish innit» What is right? | No, whales are mammals that breathe air and feed their babies milk | Yes, they breathe under water with gills like any fish | Yes, they lay eggs in the sand | No, they are giant squid
test: A sperm whale comes up with fresh round marks on its head. What most likely happened? | It fought a squid in the deep and the suckers marked it | It bumped into a ship | It slept on sharp rocks that had round holes all over them | It caught a cold
test: «bloody hell, a starfish just turned its stomach inside out on a mussel» What is going on? | It is eating, digesting the mussel outside its body | It is sick and dying | It is laying eggs | It is breathing out through its stomach on the rock
test: Why would scientists be surprised to find sponges under nine hundred metres of ice? | They live far from open water and sunlight, where food is very scarce | Sponges only live in hot, dry deserts | Sponges cannot live in water | Ice is far too hot for sponges, they like boiling water
test: In an old cartoon a sailor gets swallowed whole by a giant whale and lives inside it. Which is true? | A blue whale's throat is far too narrow to swallow a person | Blue whales swallow whole ships every year | Every whale has a little room inside it with a table and some chairs | Whales keep people as pets
test: «humpbacks all sing the same song forever, right» What is right? | No, the songs change and whales across an ocean pick up the new ones | Yes, the song never changes | Yes, only one whale sings, the same song for ever | No, humpbacks cannot make any sounds, they just splash
exam: Identify the organism which is acknowledged to be the largest animal ever to have existed. | The blue whale | The colossal squid | The largest of the dinosaurs | The southern elephant seal
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the means by which a starfish digests its prey? | The stomach is extended outside the body and digestion occurs externally | Food is masticated by means of numerous teeth | Prey is absorbed exclusively through the arms | Food is dissolved by venom injected through the spines
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Antarctic Circumpolar Current. | It encircles Antarctica and is the strongest ocean current on Earth | It is a freshwater river flowing beneath the thick ice of Antarctica | It flows only during the southern winter | It is a current of air rather than of water
exam: Ascertain the manner in which whales contribute to the growth of phytoplankton in the Southern Ocean. | Their excrement supplies iron which the phytoplankton require | Their breath warms the surface waters | Their songs stimulate the growth of plants upon the sea floor | Their bodies shade the water from excessive light
